  "findings": [
    "The claim remains OPEN. I obtained neither a proof nor an accessible counterexample.",
    "Under the stated uniform weak-composition law, fixing a block beginning after j pairs and writing n_s = R - 2j - 2L, the joint non-low positive-resonance weight is exactly T_R(K)^(-1) times the sum over h,w of 1{z_j(h) >= eta} 1{rho_{j,L}(h)} binom(h+2j-1,2j-1) a_L(w) binom(K-h-w+n_s-1,n_s-1), where T_R(K)=binom(K+R-1,R-1), a_L(w)=[x^w](sum_{b=1}^{B_0}(b+1)x^b)^L, and rho_{j,L}(h) is the stated positive amplified-resonance indicator. This follows by independently counting prefix compositions, the bounded-positive block, and suffix compositions; boundary cases require the usual zero-component convention.",
    "This identity is only a weighted finite sum. It supplies no uniform upper bound on the set of h for which rho_{j,L}(h)=1. Full multiplicative-order information for powers of 2 modulo powers of 3 does not control the short accessible h-window, and the displayed reduction does not control dependence between different block starts.",
    "The first missing resonance-specific estimate is the following strictly narrower masking bound. Define N_G as the number of grid blocks that are bounded-positive and non-low, and N_res as the number of those blocks that additionally satisfy amplified resonance. For some fixed A >= 4/lambda and C, uniformly over all sufficiently large accessible arrays and the exact conditioning data, one needs P(N_res > N_G/2 and N_G >= A log R | data) <= C/R. This does not establish abundance of N_G and therefore is strictly weaker than the target.",
    "No supplied calculation proves this masking inequality. A per-start resonance estimate followed by a union bound is insufficient for the required global O(1/R) scale. Independently, the lower-tail estimate that N_G is at least logarithmic with probability 1-O(1/R) also remains unproved because low-state occupation is not rare."
  ],
